WEBVTT
TITLE The Uniform Distribution: Equal Size Means Equal Probability — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A fair die is the discrete uniform on 1 to 6: six outcomes, each with probability 1/6 = 0.166667.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The continuous uniform on [2,8] spreads probability evenly along an interval. Its density is the constant 1/(b-a) = 1/6 = 0.166667.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Two different objects: mass on six points, against density on every point of an interval. Both are called uniform, and the word alone does not say which.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The continuous mean is (a+b)/2 = 5 and its variance is (b-a)^2/12 = 3. The width sets the spread; the location only shifts it.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The CDF of U(2,8) is F(x) = (x-2)/6 between the endpoints: a straight ramp from 0 to 1. A probability is the rise of the ramp.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
P(3 <= X <= 5.5) = (5.5-3)/6 = 0.416667. On a uniform, an interval probability is its length divided by the total length.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
For a continuous uniform P(X = 4) = 0 exactly: a single point has zero width, so it carries no probability. The ramp never jumps.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
For the die, P(X = 4) = 1/6 = 0.166667. The same word, two different answers to the question "what is the chance of exactly four".

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Take U uniform on [0,1]. Then a + (b-a)U is uniform on [a,b]. One multiplication and one shift turn the standard uniform into any other.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
With a = 2 and b = 8 the map is 2 + 6U. Over 2000 evenly spaced draws the transformed values have mean 5.0000 and variance 3.0000.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The moments transform the same way: the mean becomes a + (b-a)/2 and the variance (b-a)^2/12. A shift does not change spread; a scale does, and it squares.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Run it backwards: (X-a)/(b-a) is uniform on [0,1]. Every uniform question can be answered on [0,1] and then scaled.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Two moments determine the two endpoints. Mean 10 gives a + b = 20; variance 4/3 gives (b-a)^2 = 12 x 4/3 = 16, so b - a = 4.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Adding gives 2b = 24, so b = 12 and a = 8. The model puts both moments back into the recovered interval and gets 10 and 4/3.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
A second instance: mean 3 and variance 3/4 give a + b = 6 and b - a = 3, so a = 1.5 and b = 4.5.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
This is the reverse of the usual question, and it is the one that appears when the endpoints are unknown. Two equations, two unknowns, no guessing.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A random number generator gives U on [0,1). The integer floor(6U) + 1 is uniform on 1 to 6: that is how a computer rolls a fair die.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Over 60000 evenly spaced draws the six buckets hold exactly 10000 each, so every face has probability 0.166667.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Feed the same rule a non-uniform input and fairness dies. With U squared, floor(6U^2) + 1 lands on face 1 in 24495 of 60000 draws: 0.408250 instead of 0.166667.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
0.408250 is 1/sqrt(6) = 0.408248: face 1 wins exactly when U is below that. The transform is only as good as the input it is given.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Draw n independent uniforms and sort them. The largest is below x exactly when every draw is, so P(max <= x) = x^n.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The smallest exceeds x exactly when every draw does, so P(min > x) = (1-x)^n. For n = 3, E[max] = 3/4 and E[min] = 1/4.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The k-th smallest has mean k/(n+1). For n = 4 the four expected positions are 0.2, 0.4, 0.6 and 0.8: the sample spreads itself evenly.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The page checks these by averaging over a grid of draws as well as by the formulas, and the two routes agree to four decimals.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Three points cut the unit interval into four gaps. By symmetry every gap has the same expected length, and the four must sum to 1, so each is 1/4.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
That one picture gives E[min] = 1/(n+1), E[max] = n/(n+1) and the expected range (n-1)/(n+1) = 1/2 for n = 3, with no calculus.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
One cut, two pieces: the longer piece has expected length 3/4, because the longer piece is 1 minus the shorter.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The piece containing the point 0.4 has expected length 0.74 = 1/2 + p - p^2 at p = 0.4. Both quantities are computed here because a 2025 item uses one of them.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The CDF of any continuous variable is itself uniform on [0,1]. Read it backwards and you can manufacture any distribution from a uniform one.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Set F(x) = u and solve for x. For the exponential with rate 2, F(x) = 1 - e^(-2x), so x = -ln(1-u)/2.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Over a grid of U values, the fraction with -ln(1-u)/2 at most 0.5 is 0.6321, which is 1 - e^(-1) to four places. The transform lands where the formula says.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Take the square root instead: X = sqrt(U) has P(X <= x) = x^2, so its density is 2x and rises. Uniform in, anything out.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Pick a point by choosing a radius uniformly in [0,R] and an angle uniformly. The result is not uniform on the disk.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The inner half-radius circle holds a quarter of the area but half the points: probability 0.5 against area fraction 0.25.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Uniform on a region means equal area gets equal probability. The radius must be drawn with density 2r/R^2, not flat, because the ring at radius r is longer.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
This is the general warning: "chosen at random" is not a distribution until you say what is uniform. A uniform bit string is not a uniform integer.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: decide whether the object is discrete or continuous. That decides mass against density, and it decides whether P(X = c) is zero.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: find the two endpoints or the two integers. Step three: convert the question into a length or a count.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step four: if the question mentions a maximum, a minimum or a gap, use the order-statistic formulas rather than integrating.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Four traps: rounding a non-uniform input, forgetting the positive root, confusing the piece containing a point with the longer piece, and assuming "at random" means uniform on whatever you pictured.

